WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.700
We were in a store. and a guy in that
store told us to put our uniforms on.

2
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.100
"What the hell are you talking about?"

3
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.800
He says. "The U.S.A.'s
in a war with Japan."

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.600
We couldn't believe it.

5
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.800
Our country was attacked.
It's a different...

6
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.300
It wasn't like Korea or Vietnam.
We was attacked.

7
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.600
And. you know. it was a feeling of... .

8
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.700
Maybe we're just dumb country
people where I come from...

9
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.100
...but a lot of us volunteered.

10
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.900
"Who would like to volunteer
for the Tank Corps? For the Air Force?

11
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.300
For the Navy?" Or whatever.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.500
Then they said. "Who would
volunteer for the Airborne?"

13
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.700
So I says.
"What the hell's the Airborne?"

14
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.400
We came from a small. small town...

15
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.900
...and three fellows in that town
that were 4-F committed suicide.

16
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.200
Because they couldn't go.
A different time.

17
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.000
I did things. I didn't do them
for medals or accolades.

18
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.300
I did them because they... .
It's what had to be done.

19
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.600
The guy says. "You jump out
of airplanes to fight the enemy."

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.600
These guys said. "Go to hell."
Nobody put up their hands.

21
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.500
And I don't know
what brought it up...

22
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:38.200
...but the guy speaking said.
"You get paid $50 a month more."

23
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.800
So that made it 100 bucks.

24
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

25
00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.300
Okay. boys. let's get
this gear stowed.

26
00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.000
Fox Company. we go in five.

27
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.500
- Sergeant Maynard?
- Nope.

28
00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.300
In the name of the Father
and the Son... .

29
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:27.300
1st Platoon. draw small arms.

30
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.400
It'll be too late when
you face the enemy.

31
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.700
Easy Company!

32
00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.900
Listen up! Gather up around me!

33
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.900
Move it up! Come on!

34
00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.400
Let's go!

35
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.900
The Channel coast is socked in
with rain and fog.

36
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.900
High winds on the drop zone.
No jump tonight.

37
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.800
The invasion has been postponed.
We're on a 24-hour stand-down.

38
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.900
- Drill sergeants. take charge.
- 1st Platoon... .

39
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:04.400
Well. this is quite a surprise.

40
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.500
Not particularly. It so happens
I rather expected it.

41
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.200
Well. I can't see how you people
can pass up $80.000 for the cause.

42
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.400
For whose cause?

43
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:21.500
If you're so interested in serving
a cause. why don't you join the Army?

44
00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.300
- I'm 4-F.
- You look 1-A to me.

45
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.000
You don't look so bad yourself.

46
00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.100
Well. it's my arteries.

47
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.700
Should you be giving blood?

48
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.000
I think it's clearing up.

49
00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.000
Think it's clearing up?

50
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.200
Nope.

51
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.500
I think it's clearing up.

52
00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.600
- How are your men?
- They'll be fine.

53
00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:13.400
Five o'clock in New York.
Four o'clock in Chicago.

54
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.800
- Happy hour. huh?
- Yeah. happy hour.

55
00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:22.900
A couple of drinks. Maybe
an early dinner before the theater.

56
00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.400
Civilized place for civilized men.

57
00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:39.700
Should have been born earlier. Nix.

58
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.400
What. and give up all this?

59
00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.300
We'll go to Chicago.
I'll take you there.

60
00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:01.000
Yeah.

61
00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.800
We'll see.

62
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.900
- Actually. you know who's from there?
- Who? Oh. him.

63
00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:22.400
712 days with that son of a bitch
and here we are.

64
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.400
You people are at the position
of attention!

65
00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.000
Pvt. Perconte. did you blouse
your trousers like a paratrooper?

66
00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.500
- No. sir.
- Then explain the creases.

67
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.700
- No excuse. sir.
- Volunteering is one thing.

68
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.900
You've got a long way
to prove you belong.

69
00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.800
Your weekend pass is revoked.

70
00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.200
- Name.
- Luz. George.

71
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.300
Dirt in the rear sight aperture.
Pass revoked.

72
00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.600
When did you sew on the chevron?

73
00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.400
Yesterday. sir.

74
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.000
Long enough to notice this. Revoked.

75
00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:44.600
Sir.

76
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.800
- Name.
- Malarkey. Donald G.

77
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.100
- Isn't "malarkey" slang for bullshit?
- Yes. sir.

78
00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:58.700
Rust on the butt plate hinge spring.
Pvt. Bullshit. Revoked.

79
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.900
- Name.
- Liebgott. Joseph D.. sir.

80
00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:08.700
Rusty bayonet. Liebgott.
You wanna kill Germans?

81
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.600
- Yes. sir.
- Not with this.

82
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.500
I wouldn't take this rusty piece
of shit to war...

83
00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.900
...and I will not take you
in your condition.

84
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.700
Thanks to these men
and their infractions. every man...

85
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.000
...who had a weekend pass
has lost it.

86
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.400
Change into your PT gear.
We're running Currahee.

87
00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.500
2nd Platoon. fall out.
We have two minutes.

88
00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:47.100
I ain't going up that hill.

89
00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:51.500
Perconte. what are you thinking of.
blousing your pants?

90
00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:56.400
- Shut up. He gigged everybody.
- Don't give him no excuses.

91
00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.600
Excuses? Look at these trousers and
tell me if there's a crease on them.

92
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.400
Let's go. On the road.
in PT formation. Let's move.

93
00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.500
Perconte. Let's go. Perconte.

94
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:15.800
Pvt. White. why are you not
in your PT gear?

95
00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:23.000
I asked you a question. private.

96
00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:44.500
Easy Company. While you run.
we'll take your dames to the movies.

97
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:48.300
Good. they need some female company.

98
00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.500
- Where do we run?
- Currahee!

99
00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:58.300
- What's Currahee mean?
- "We stand alone"!

100
00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.200
- How far up? How far down?
- Three miles up. three down!

101
00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.000
- What company is this?
- Easy Company!

102
00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.500
- And what do we do?
- Stand alone!

103
00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.900
Do not help that man!
Do not help that man! Do not stop!

104
00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:18.100
You have 13 minutes to get
to the top of this mountain...

105
00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:23.900
...if you want to serve
in the paratroopers. Hi-yo. Silver!

106
00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.900
Come on. You can make it.

107
00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.600
Come on. Alley. Come on. Guarnere.
You got it! Come on!

108
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.200
Never thought I'd see the day.
Private Wynn.

109
00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.300
We are coming on 23 minutes.

110
00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:04.800
That may be good enough
for the rest of the 506...

111
00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.800
...but that is not good
enough for Easy Company!

112
00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.900
We can do this! Come on!

113
00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:23.900
Come on.

114
00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.600
Come on.

115
00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.900
I'm gonna say something.

116
00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.500
To who?

117
00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.500
Lt. Winters.

118
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.000
- What is it?
- Permission to speak. sir.

119
00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:06.300
- Permission granted.
- Sir. we've got nine companies. sir.

120
00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.000
We do.

121
00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:14.700
Why are we the only one marching every
Friday. 12 miles. in the pitch dark?

122
00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:20.400
- Why do you think. Pvt. Randleman?
- Lt. Sobel hates us. sir.

123
00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:28.800
Lt. Sobel does not hate Easy Company.
Pvt. Randleman.

124
00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.300
He just hates you.

125
00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:33.800
Thank you. sir.

126
00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:39.500
- He hates him back.
- He hates you too. Muck.

127
00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:43.200
Lt. Winters. I want canteens out.
caps unscrewed.

128
00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.300
Canteens out and open.

129
00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.500
They will pour the contents
onto the ground.

130
00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.000
You will upend your canteen.

131
00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.000
- Now. lieutenant.
- Pour them!

132
00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:01.500
Who is this?

133
00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.200
Christenson! Why is there
no water in your canteen?

134
00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.500
You drank from your canteen?

135
00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:09.300
- Lt. Winters!
- Yes. sir.

136
00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:13.900
Was this man ordered
to not drink from his canteen?

137
00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:17.400
- He was. sir.
- You disobeyed a direct order.

138
00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:22.300
You will fill your canteen
and repeat all 12 miles of the march.

139
00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:25.200
- Yes. sir.
- Fall out!

140
00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.600
What are you doing
with my company?

141
00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:40.500
You're late and you allow troopers
to disobey orders?

142
00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:42.800
No excuse. sir.

143
00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:47.500
You're making me look bad.
This is not Dog Company.

144
00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:52.600
This is not Fox Company.
This is Easy Company.

145
00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:57.100
Under my command. this will be
the first and finest company.

146
00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:02.000
I want the names of six men...

147
00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:06.700
...their infractions and your
disciplinary recommendations by 0130.

148
00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:08.700
Is that clear?

149
00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.700
What infractions. sir?

150
00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.500
Find some.

151
00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.300
So. what did you do?

152
00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:23.200
Picked six men
and gave them latrine duty.

153
00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:24.600
The lucky six?

154
00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:31.000
McDonald. Toye. Perconte.
Lipton. Muck and Guarnere.

155
00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.600
- Why them?
- It was their turn.

156
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:36.700
Sobel's a genius.

157
00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.500
I had a headmaster like him
in school. I know the type.

158
00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:44.500
Lewis. Michelangelo's a genius.
Beethoven's a genius.

159
00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:48.200
You know a man who wouldn't
double-time Currahee...

160
00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:51.500
...just to piss in that guy's
morning coffee?

161
00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.700
Let's go. let's go.

162
00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.200
- Go!
- 1000. 2000. 3000. 4000... .

163
00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:57.900
Stand in the door.

164
00:16:58.200 --> 00:16:59.900
- Go!
- 1000. 2000... .

165
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.800
You just broke both your legs. Gordon.

166
00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:05.900
- Are you trying to get killed?
- No. sir!

167
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.200
Stand in the door.

168
00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:24.000
Shit.

169
00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:34.300
To the wall! Over as a team.
Come on. against the wall!

170
00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.000
Jesus. what the hell is this?

171
00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.500
That's pig guts. boy.

172
00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.000
Goddamn it.

173
00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.700
- Why are you here?
- To be in the Airborne.

174
00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.300
- I don't believe you. Why?
- To be in the Airborne.

175
00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:58.100
You have 50 minutes to the top and
back. and I will be watching you.

176
00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:00.800
What are you waiting for?

177
00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:46.200
Easy Company has the best performance
record in the 2nd Battalion.

178
00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:49.900
I believe every bit of that
is thanks to you.

179
00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:54.300
- Congratulations. Captain Sobel.
- Thank you. sir.

180
00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:58.800
Isn't that Lt. Winters leading
Easy Company in PT?

181
00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.500
Yes. sir.

182
00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.500
He's a good man. I was planning
on giving him these today.

183
00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:08.900
Why don't you do it?
He'd be proud to get them from you.

184
00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.400
Pornography. contraband.

185
00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:23.600
Non-regulation clothing. contraband.

186
00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:28.500
This man had 200 prophylactic kits
in his footlocker.

187
00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:33.500
How in the name of God was he gonna
have the strength to fight the war?

188
00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:40.200
How is it Pvt. Tipper has spare time
for so much correspondence?

189
00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.900
Captain. are personal letters
to be considered contraband?

190
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:51.700
These men aren't paratroopers yet.
They have no personal property.

191
00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:56.800
What is this?

192
00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:59.100
Anybody.

193
00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.500
It's a can of peaches.

194
00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.500
Lt. Nixon thinks this
is a can of peaches.

195
00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:09.100
That is incorrect.
Your weekend pass is cancelled.

196
00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:13.700
This is United States Army property...

197
00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:18.400
...which was taken without
authorization from my mess facility.

198
00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:23.400
And I will not tolerate thievery
in my unit. Whose footlocker is this?

199
00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:25.700
Private Parks'. sir.

200
00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:28.300
Get rid of him.

201
00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:33.800
All weekend passes are cancelled.
officers included.

202
00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.900
Carry on.

203
00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:42.800
Lt. Winters.

204
00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:54.200
Colonel Sink has seen fit
to promote you.

205
00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:58.600
As first lieutenant. you'll serve
as my executive officer.

206
00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:01.500
- Congratulations.
- Thank you. sir.

207
00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:07.800
And as a test of your organizational
skills and command potential...

208
00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:13.500
...I am designating you
mess officer for 14 days.

209
00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.700
Report to the mess kitchen
at 0515 hours.

210
00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:20.600
Company breakfast
to be served at 0600.

211
00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.500
Yes. sir.

212
00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:27.100
And. Dick. there's rain
forecast tomorrow.

213
00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:33.400
The company will have a light afternoon
of lecture and classroom instruction.

214
00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:39.900
A special meal before their afternoon
off would be a welcome change of pace.

215
00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.900
- Would you agree?
- Yes. sir.

216
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.700
I like spaghetti.

217
00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:57.000
Hold on. more coming.

218
00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:00.100
These guys are packing it away.

219
00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:13.800
It's orange. Spaghetti ain't
supposed to be orange.

220
00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:17.600
This ain't spaghetti.
It's Army noodles with ketchup.

221
00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:19.500
You ain't gotta eat it.

222
00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:24.100
As an Italian. you know calling
this spaghetti is a mortal sin.

223
00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:26.300
- I'll eat it.
- I'm eating!

224
00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:28.300
Get out of here!

225
00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
Orders changed! Get up!

226
00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:37.800
Lectures are cancelled! Easy Company
is running up Currahee! Move!

227
00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.700
Three miles up. three miles down.

228
00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:46.800
Hi-yo. Silver!

229
00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:49.400
Let's go! Let's go!

230
00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:58.000
You're a washout. Pvt. Hoobler.
You should pack up and go home!

231
00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:03.700
Looks like Gordon's done. Finished?
You do not deserve to get your wings.

232
00:23:04.100 --> 00:23:06.600
Pvt. Randleman. you look tired.

233
00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:10.000
There's an ambulance waiting for you.

234
00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.500
It can all be over. right now.
No more pain...

235
00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:17.200
...no more Currahee.
no more Captain Sobel.

236
00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:02.400
- Are we ready to be Army paratroopers?
- Yes. sergeant!

237
00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:04.000
I hope so.

238
00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:08.900
This will be the first of five exits
from a C-47 aircraft set for today.

239
00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:10.800
Get ready!

240
00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:14.200
Stand up! Hook up!

241
00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:19.500
Upon completion of your final jump.
you'll be certified paratroopers.

242
00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:21.300
Check equipment!

243
00:24:23.600 --> 00:24:26.100
Sound off for equipment check!

244
00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:27.800
Nine okay!

245
00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:30.700
Eight okay! Seven okay!

246
00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:32.900
Six okay!

247
00:24:33.100 --> 00:24:35.700
Five okay! Four okay!

248
00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:39.000
Three okay! Two okay! One okay!

249
00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:44.600
There'll be many men jumping today.
hopefully under deployed canopies.

250
00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.300
Stand in the door!

251
00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:53.600
I guarantee you're gonna love it.
lieutenant! Go! Go!

252
00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:59.200
Jumping from 1000 feet AGL.
in sticks of 12 jumpers per aircraft.

253
00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:03.300
You just have to remember
what you were taught.

254
00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.600
I guarantee gravity will
take care of the rest.

255
00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.000
Go! Go! Go!

256
00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:52.500
Damn it.

257
00:25:53.700 --> 00:25:59.300
Gentlemen. rest assured. any refusals
in the aircraft or at the door...

258
00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:04.100
...and I guarantee you will be
out of the Airborne.

259
00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:09.200
Four okay! Three okay!
Two okay! One okay!

260
00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:11.400
Stand in the door!

261
00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:15.100
Now!

262
00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:29.600
1000. 2000. 3000. 4000. 5000...

263
00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:33.500
...6000. 7000. 8000. 9000.

264
00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.100
Yeah!

265
00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.500
Hi-yo. Silver!

266
00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:49.500
If you had style like me. somebody
might mistake you for somebody.

267
00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.700
You mean like your fucking sergeant?

268
00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:55.800
I'm just kidding.

269
00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.700
- Congratulations. Martin.
- Cpl. Toye.

270
00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.300
There will be no leaning
in my company.

271
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.600
Are those dusty jump wings?

272
00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:11.600
How will you slay the Huns
with dust on your jump wings?

273
00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:14.300
Luz. just give me a drink.

274
00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:18.000
Hell of an idea. Joe.

275
00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:19.800
There you go.

276
00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:24.100
Three miles up. three miles down.

277
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:26.800
Ten-hut!

278
00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:38.600
Well. at ease. paratroopers.

279
00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:42.900
- Good evening. Easy Company.
- Evening. sir!

280
00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:47.000
Parachute Infantry is a new concept
in military history.

281
00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:50.800
But the 506 will forge
that new concept into victory.

282
00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:52.400
Yes. sir!

283
00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:56.200
I want you to know I'm damn proud
of every one of you.

284
00:27:56.600 --> 00:27:59.100
Now. you deserve this party.

285
00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:01.200
- Thanks. Sgt. Grant.
- Sir.

286
00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:05.100
So I want you to have fun.
and remember our motto:

287
00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:08.000
- Currahee!
- Currahee!

288
00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:36.500
Petty!

289
00:28:36.700 --> 00:28:38.300
Map! Come on!

290
00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.800
Oh. Christ.

291
00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:56.100
We're in the wrong position.

292
00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:02.200
- We're in the wrong position.
- We're in position for ambush.

293
00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:05.400
Let the enemy come
into our killing zone.

294
00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:08.600
They're right out there.
Let's get them.

295
00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:13.400
- Sir. we have perfect cover here.
- Lieutenant. deploy your troops.

296
00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:18.000
2nd Platoon. move out.

297
00:29:18.500 --> 00:29:20.700
- What?
- Tactical column.

298
00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:44.300
You've just been killed.
along with 95% of your company.

299
00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:46.100
Your outfit?

300
00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:49.600
Easy Company.
2nd Battalion. 506.

301
00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:58.300
Leave three wounded men on the ground
and report back to the assembly area.

302
00:29:58.700 --> 00:30:00.500
Goddamn it.

303
00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:07.400
You. You. You.

304
00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.200
What are you gonna do?

305
00:30:12.500 --> 00:30:15.500
Nothing. just keep training the men.

306
00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:18.100
Am I interrupting?

307
00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.300
No. no.

308
00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:25.400
Lt. Lewis Nixon. Lt. Harry Welsh.
just in from the 82nd.

309
00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:29.600
- Congratulations on the promotion.
- If you wanna call it that.

310
00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:34.400
You'll learn him pretty quickly.
No flaws. vices or sense of humor.

311
00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:38.000
Just like your chums
up at Battalion Staff?

312
00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:41.700
- What's up?
- I'm hearing a lot of rumblings.

313
00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:45.100
Sobel? We were just
talking about that.

314
00:30:45.400 --> 00:30:48.100
So he gets a little jumpy
in the field.

315
00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:52.000
- He gets jumpy and you get killed.
- That's nice.

316
00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.800
Listen. if we discuss it.
it should be among ourselves.

317
00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:57.800
Absolutely.

318
00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:08.000
- 2nd Platoon ready?
- Ready. sir.

319
00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:12.400
Then get them in formation.
We're moving out.

320
00:31:12.700 --> 00:31:14.100
Yes. sir.

321
00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:43.400
Goddamn. you gotta admit.
He's got no chance.

322
00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:47.400
Either the Krauts will get him.
or one of us.

323
00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:49.400
Who? Sobel?

324
00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:51.400
He screwed up one maneuver.

325
00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:54.000
I'm always fumbling with grenades.

326
00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.200
It would be easy if one
went off by accident.

327
00:31:57.500 --> 00:32:01.000
They must have put him
in charge for a reason.

328
00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:05.000
Because the Army wouldn't
make a mistake. right. Shift?

329
00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:22.000
- Going my way?
- Where the train takes me.

330
00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:24.400
- Where's that?
- I haven't a clue.

331
00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:28.900
Yeah. come on. Take a guess.
Atlantic. Pacific. Atlantic?

332
00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:32.100
I'm not the intelligence officer.

333
00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:36.300
As such. I know. but if I told you.
I'd have to kill you.

334
00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:38.400
So don't tell me.

335
00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:45.200
New York City. Troop ship. England.

336
00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:51.100
We're invading Europe. my friend.

337
00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:53.100
Fortress Europa.

338
00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:57.000
Since when do I drink?

339
00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:02.000
If I thought you'd drink.
I wouldn't offer it to you.

340
00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:07.500
Nix. what are you gonna do
when you get into combat?

341
00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:14.100
Oh. I have every confidence
in my scrounging abilities.

342
00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:18.600
And I have a case of Vat 69
hidden in your footlocker.

343
00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:24.900
- Really?
- Oh. yeah.

344
00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:28.200
Morning.

345
00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:33.000
This could turn into a real nice trip.

346
00:33:48.900 --> 00:33:50.900
Dear Sir or Madam:

347
00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:56.400
Soon your son will drop from the sky
to engage and defeat the enemy.

348
00:33:56.800 --> 00:34:02.200
Your letters of love and encouragement
will arm him with a fighting heart.

349
00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:08.200
With that. he cannot fail but will win
glory for himself. make you proud...

350
00:34:08.700 --> 00:34:12.800
...and his country grateful
for his service in its hour of need.

351
00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:16.800
Signed Herbert M. Sobel.
Captain. Commanding.

352
00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:34.300
Right now. some lucky bastard's
headed for the South Pacific.

353
00:34:34.700 --> 00:34:37.700
He'll get billeted
on some tropical island.

354
00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:41.000
Sitting under a tree
with naked native girls...

355
00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:45.500
...helping him cut up coconuts
so he can hand-feed the flamingos.

356
00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:50.000
- Flamingos are mean. They bite.
- So do the naked native girls.

357
00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:51.600
With any luck.

358
00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:54.100
Guys. I'm glad I'm going to Europe.

359
00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:57.000
Hitler gets this
across the windpipe.

360
00:34:57.300 --> 00:35:00.500
Roosevelt changes Thanksgiving
to Joe Toye Day...

361
00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:04.500
...and pays me $ 10.000 a year
for the rest of my life.

362
00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:08.200
What if they send us to North Africa?

363
00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:12.500
- My brother's there. He says it's hot.
- It's hot in Africa?

364
00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:16.000
Shut up.
Point is. it don't matter where we go.

365
00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:21.200
Once we get into combat. you trust
only yourself and the man next to you.

366
00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.600
- Long as he's a paratrooper.
- Yeah?

367
00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:27.900
What if that paratrooper
turns out to be Sobel?

368
00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:31.600
If I'm next to Sobel.
I'm moving down the line.

369
00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:35.500
Hook up with another officer.
like Heyliger or Winters.

370
00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:41.700
I like Winters. But when bullets fly.
I don't want a Quaker fighting for me.

371
00:35:42.100 --> 00:35:45.100
- How do you know that?
- He ain't Catholic.

372
00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:49.600
- Neither's Sobel.
- That prick's a son of Abraham.

373
00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:52.600
- He's what?
- He's a Jew.

374
00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:54.400
Fuck.

375
00:35:56.700 --> 00:35:59.700
- I'm a Jew.
- Congratulations.

376
00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:02.800
Get your nose out of my face.

377
00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:11.400
What's all that about?

378
00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:13.600
Gonorrhea called Sobel a Jew.

379
00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:17.000
Liebgott took offense
because he's a Jew too.

380
00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:21.300
Fighting over Sobel. That's smart.

381
00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.300
Attack!

382
00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:46.100
No! You wanna kill him!

383
00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:49.400
Parry right! Parry left!
Front!

384
00:36:49.700 --> 00:36:50.900
Recover.

385
00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:52.200
Okay.

386
00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:58.400
We talked about magnetic declination
and the left-add. right-subtract rule.

387
00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:00.500
Today we'll use it.

388
00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:03.600
There are two basic
fighting positions.

389
00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:06.300
The first is a prepared position.

390
00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:12.000
This position's advantages are that
it gives you cover and concealment.

391
00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:14.500
Commence fire!

392
00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:21.000
We'll maneuver through these trees.

393
00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:26.100
At the same time. our 2nd Platoon.
in this case. moves over here.

394
00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:29.600
He'll then kill or capture
that German.

395
00:37:54.800 --> 00:37:56.400
Sobel's late.

396
00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:10.700
Why is there a fence here?

397
00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:15.400
There should be no fence here.
Tipper!

398
00:38:15.700 --> 00:38:17.900
Give me the map.

399
00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:23.400
Perconte. Luz.
Get the men. Get them ba...

400
00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:28.700
- Take cover behind those trees.
- All right. Let's go. Move it out.

401
00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:34.900
There should...
There should be no fence.

402
00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:37.500
We could go over it. sir.

403
00:38:37.700 --> 00:38:41.000
Really? That's not the point.
Where the goddamn...?

404
00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:44.000
Where the goddamn hell are we?

405
00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:46.200
- Perconte.
- Yeah?

406
00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:48.600
- Sobel's lost again. right?
- Yeah.

407
00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.000
- Fucking Christ.
- Hey. Luz?

408
00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:56.500
Can you do Major Horton?

409
00:38:56.800 --> 00:38:59.900
Does a wild bear crap
in the woods. son?

410
00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:04.300
Maybe the major can goose
this schmuck. Get us moving?

411
00:39:04.700 --> 00:39:06.900
- No way. I'm not gonna...
- Oh. yes.

412
00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:09.400
Luz. you gotta. Come on.

413
00:39:09.700 --> 00:39:11.900
All right. just this once.

414
00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:20.500
356833.
Isn't that the intersection?

415
00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:24.100
No. sir. it's here.
You're a full grid off.

416
00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:25.700
Goddamn it.

417
00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:31.200
- Is there a problem. Captain Sobel?
- Who said that? Who broke silence?

418
00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:34.200
I think it's Major Horton. sir.

419
00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:39.400
Major Horton? What. is he...?
Did he join us?

420
00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:42.200
Maybe he's moving
between the platoons.

421
00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:46.900
What is the goddamn holdup.
Mr. Sobel?!

422
00:39:48.400 --> 00:39:51.800
A fence. sir. a...
God!

423
00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:56.600
- A barbed-wire fence.
- Oh. that dog just ain't gonna hunt.

424
00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:58.400
Shut up!

425
00:39:58.600 --> 00:40:03.000
Now. you cut that fence and get this
goddamn platoon on the move!

426
00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:06.400
Yes. sir!

427
00:40:08.600 --> 00:40:10.700
- Where are my wire cutters?
- Sir.

428
00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:17.100
- We have to move.
- Without Sobel and 1st Platoon?

429
00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:19.900
It's a T-intersection. We improvise.

430
00:40:20.200 --> 00:40:23.800
Lay down fire to cut the road
in all directions.

431
00:40:24.400 --> 00:40:28.900
Go right with 1st Squad. Tell 2nd
to go left. I'll be center with 3rd.

432
00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:31.400
- Go.
- Yes. sir.

433
00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:55.200
Dearie me.

434
00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:07.800
Bloody hell.

435
00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:31.200
You've done it now. Yanks.
You've captured me.

436
00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:38.600
Hi-yo. Silver!

437
00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:43.600
Would that be the enemy?

438
00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:46.300
As a matter of fact. yes.

439
00:41:53.700 --> 00:41:56.700
Good work. 2nd Platoon.
We took the objective.

440
00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:08.800
Who was the idiot
who cut that man's fence?

441
00:42:09.100 --> 00:42:11.000
- I was ordered to.
- By who?

442
00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:13.400
- Maj. Horton. sir.
- Maj. Horton?

443
00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:15.800
- Yes.
- He told you to do that?

444
00:42:16.100 --> 00:42:19.900
- Yes. sir.
- He ordered you to cut the fence?

445
00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:21.900
Yes. he did.

446
00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:26.200
Major Horton is on leave in London.

447
00:42:28.400 --> 00:42:30.600
Get those cows out of here.

448
00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:00.800
Lt. Winters.

449
00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:06.700
With Capt. Sobel's compliments. sir.

450
00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:10.600
Lieutenant.

451
00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:34.200
- Oh. for crying out loud.
- Misspelled "court-martial."

452
00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:41.400
No. sir. I do not understand.

453
00:43:41.600 --> 00:43:44.900
Your orders were to inspect
latrines at 1000 hours.

454
00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:51.200
From 0930 to 0955. I was censoring
mail by order of Col. Strayer.

455
00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:54.400
At 1000 hours. I followed your orders.

456
00:43:54.700 --> 00:43:57.300
I changed that time to 0945.

457
00:43:57.600 --> 00:43:59.600
- No one told me.
- I telephoned.

458
00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:02.000
The family I'm with has no phone.

459
00:44:02.300 --> 00:44:04.900
- And sent a runner.
- No runner found me.

460
00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:08.700
When given a task to perform
by a ranking officer...

461
00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:13.400
...you should have delegated
latrine inspection to another officer.

462
00:44:13.700 --> 00:44:15.300
You failed to do so.

463
00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:21.100
Were I to let this go unpunished. what
kind of message is that to the men?

464
00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:25.300
- I performed my duty as I was ordered.
- And I disagree.

465
00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:29.500
So your options are
quite simple. lieutenant.

466
00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:34.200
Punishment will be denial
of a 48-hour pass for 60 days.

467
00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:37.300
Stand before me at attention.

468
00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:44.500
Or you may initiate an appeal
and request a trial by court-martial.

469
00:44:49.200 --> 00:44:53.700
You spend weekends on the base anyway.
Be a man. Take the punishment.

470
00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:56.900
May I borrow your pen. sir?

471
00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:10.200
My endorsement. sir.

472
00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:14.600
I request trial by court-martial.

473
00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:28.200
- We lost Winters to the mess.
- You're joking.

474
00:45:28.500 --> 00:45:33.300
Strayer did it while he figures out
the procedures for his court-martial.

475
00:45:33.700 --> 00:45:36.800
- Nixon better get him out.
- And if he don't?

476
00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:41.200
Winters scrambles eggs while
we make the big jump with Sobel.

477
00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:44.000
Not me.

478
00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:48.700
- We're going through with this. right?
- We gotta do something.

479
00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:51.500
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

480
00:45:51.800 --> 00:45:53.100
All right.

481
00:45:53.700 --> 00:45:55.000
Good.

482
00:45:56.800 --> 00:46:01.700
- Let's be clear of the consequences.
- I don't care about the consequences.

483
00:46:02.300 --> 00:46:05.600
We could be lined up
against a wall and shot.

484
00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:09.500
Now. I'm ready to face that.

485
00:46:10.700 --> 00:46:13.600
And every one of us had better be too.

486
00:46:18.500 --> 00:46:22.200
I will not follow that man
into combat.

487
00:46:25.300 --> 00:46:26.900
Me neither.

488
00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:30.700
All right.

489
00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:34.000
Then let's do it.

490
00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:49.400
"I hereby...

491
00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:52.700
...no longer...

492
00:46:53.900 --> 00:46:55.800
...wish to serve...

493
00:46:58.100 --> 00:47:00.500
...as a non-commissioned officer...

494
00:47:07.200 --> 00:47:09.000
...in Easy Company."

495
00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.800
All right. boys. Good luck.

496
00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:33.300
I ought to have you all shot!

497
00:47:33.600 --> 00:47:38.300
This is an act of mutiny while
we prepare for the invasion of Europe.

498
00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:40.700
- Sgt. Harris?
- Sir.

499
00:47:41.000 --> 00:47:42.400
Turn in your stripes.

500
00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:45.700
You are transferred
out of my regiment.

501
00:47:46.100 --> 00:47:48.000
- Sir.
- Get out.

502
00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:51.700
- Sgt. Ranney?
- Sir.

503
00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:55.100
You're lucky I'm only
busting you to private.

504
00:47:55.400 --> 00:47:58.700
All of you NCOs have disgraced
the 101st Airborne.

505
00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:03.900
You're lucky we're on the eve of
the largest action in war history...

506
00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:09.300
...which leaves me no choice
but to spare your lives.

507
00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:14.900
Now. get out of my office
and out of my sight.

508
00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:16.400
Get.

509
00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:35.700
Keep them coming. Thattaboy.
That's it.

510
00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:11.700
I can only speculate.
Most men would never do this.

511
00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:15.900
But I believe just a few
of the sergeants may have felt...

512
00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:20.400
...their loyalty lay more
to the platoon than to the company.

513
00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:25.300
And these few convinced the other
NCOs to turn in their stripes?

514
00:49:25.700 --> 00:49:28.800
As staff sergeants.
they have a great influence.

515
00:49:29.100 --> 00:49:32.800
But the rest are good men.
I can work with them.

516
00:49:39.500 --> 00:49:43.800
Winters' court-martial has been
an unpleasant distraction.

517
00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:46.400
Indeed it has. sir.

518
00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:51.400
However. your command of Easy
Company has been exemplary.

519
00:49:51.800 --> 00:49:53.400
Thank you. sir.

520
00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:58.900
In fact. except for the actions
of a few of your non-coms...

521
00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:03.600
...you fielded one of the finest
companies of soldiers I've seen.

522
00:50:03.900 --> 00:50:05.300
Yes. sir.

523
00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:11.100
Division has established a parachute
training school at Chilton Foliat.

524
00:50:11.500 --> 00:50:15.300
The idea is for non-infantry types...

525
00:50:15.700 --> 00:50:19.400
...vital to the invasion.
such as doctors and chaplains...

526
00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:22.300
...to take jump training there.

527
00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:28.100
I can't think of anyone more qualified
to command such a school than you.

528
00:50:31.100 --> 00:50:32.400
Sir?

529
00:50:35.900 --> 00:50:38.900
I'm reassigning you to Chilton Foliat.

530
00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:49.300
I'm losing Easy Company?

531
00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:52.600
The war effort needs you elsewhere.

532
00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:01.500
- Permission to speak. sir.
- Granted.

533
00:51:05.700 --> 00:51:08.300
Is...? Who will be replacing me?

534
00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:12.200
Lt. Meehan from Baker Company
is senior.

535
00:51:14.200 --> 00:51:18.300
Good luck at Chilton Foliat. Herbert.
Don't let us down. now.

536
00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:26.200
No. sir.

537
00:51:33.200 --> 00:51:34.700
Carry on.

538
00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:33.000
2nd Platoon. listen up. I want
the 1st Squad in A-side tents there.

539
00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:35.900
3rd Squad. second row... .

540
00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:52.000
Holy shit!

541
00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:56.300
No. it's all right.
We're Tommies. not Boche.

542
00:52:56.600 --> 00:52:58.800
- Is all this real?
- Yeah. yeah.

543
00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:04.200
It's for you lads. so you can get your
mince pies on some Jerry clobber.

544
00:53:04.700 --> 00:53:07.400
- If you know what I mean.
- Not really.

545
00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:09.000
You got a Luger?

546
00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:12.800
- I'm dying to hold a real Luger.
- Yeah. go on. then.

547
00:53:14.200 --> 00:53:16.400
Quick butchers. yeah?

548
00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:22.700
- She's a doozy.
- That's pukka. isn't it?

549
00:53:23.200 --> 00:53:24.900
- What?
- Eh?

550
00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:27.400
Hey. Petty!

551
00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:29.200
Hey. mate?

552
00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:34.400
You're having a bath if you think
you're half-inching that.

553
00:53:34.700 --> 00:53:37.100
Oh. yeah. Sorry. Well. good luck.

554
00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:39.000
You too. mate.

555
00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:41.600
What's up. Hoobs?

556
00:53:42.700 --> 00:53:46.400
These men have been through
the Army's toughest training.

557
00:53:46.700 --> 00:53:51.100
Under the worst circumstances.
And they volunteered for it.

558
00:53:51.500 --> 00:53:54.000
I was just shooting craps with them...

559
00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:56.200
You know why they volunteered?

560
00:53:56.500 --> 00:54:01.600
So when things got bad. the man
next to them would be the best.

561
00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:06.800
- Not a draftee who'll get them killed.
- Are you mad because they like me?

562
00:54:07.100 --> 00:54:09.900
Because I'm getting
to know my soldiers?

563
00:54:10.200 --> 00:54:15.200
You've been with these guys two years?
I've been here for six days.

564
00:54:15.500 --> 00:54:18.500
- You were gambling.
- So? Soldiers do that.

565
00:54:18.800 --> 00:54:22.800
- I don't deserve a reprimand for it.
- What if you'd won?

566
00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:27.800
- What?
- What if you'd won?

567
00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:37.000
Never put yourself in a position
where you can take from these men.

568
00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:43.900
- Lt. Meehan?
- Enter.

569
00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:50.000
On the last training jump... .
I had a compass.

570
00:54:51.300 --> 00:54:53.100
Close the flap.

571
00:54:55.100 --> 00:54:56.900
- Then we turned left.
- Yeah.

572
00:54:57.200 --> 00:55:01.000
Bearing 0-4-2. 12 minutes.

573
00:55:01.300 --> 00:55:03.800
- Then another left.
- Yes.

574
00:55:04.100 --> 00:55:06.500
Call it 3-5-8.

575
00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:09.800
For 10 1/2 minutes.

576
00:55:10.300 --> 00:55:13.600
- Green light. right over Ramsbury.
- Ramsbury.

577
00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:15.800
Every single time.

578
00:55:16.600 --> 00:55:20.500
Linear distance on the grid
of about... .

579
00:55:22.700 --> 00:55:24.800
Okay. Ramsbury.

580
00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:27.600
Upottery.

581
00:55:35.200 --> 00:55:36.600
So... .

582
00:55:37.200 --> 00:55:39.100
It's Normandy.

583
00:55:39.900 --> 00:55:42.700
Sainte-Marie-du-Mont.
Causeway number one.

584
00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.900
Causeway number two.
The ultimate field problem.

585
00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:52.400
The Douve River estuary divides two
beachheads. code name Utah. here...

586
00:55:52.800 --> 00:55:54.700
...and Omaha. here.

587
00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:59.600
Seaborne Infantry will hit these
beaches at a specified date and time.

588
00:56:00.500 --> 00:56:02.500
H-Hour. D-Day.

589
00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:06.600
Airborne's objective is to take
the town of Carentan...

590
00:56:06.900 --> 00:56:10.600
...linking Utah and Omaha
into a continuous beachhead.

591
00:56:11.000 --> 00:56:14.600
...linking Omaha and Utah
into one continuous beachhead.

592
00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:21.100
Each trooper will learn this operation
and know his mission to the detail.

593
00:56:21.500 --> 00:56:23.300
- Lt. Meehan?
- Yes. Dukeman.

594
00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:26.700
- Are we dropping tonight?
- We'll let you know.

595
00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:31.300
In the meantime. study these
sand tables. maps and recon photos...

596
00:56:31.700 --> 00:56:34.400
...until you can draw a map by memory.

597
00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:37.200
We'll drop behind
this Atlantic Wall...

598
00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:40.900
...five hours before the 4th Infantry
lands at Utah.

599
00:56:41.500 --> 00:56:46.300
Between our assembly area and the
objective. there is a German garrison.

600
00:56:46.700 --> 00:56:49.200
In this area. Sainte-Marie-du-Mont.

601
00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:52.900
Easy Company
will destroy that garrison.

602
00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:59.400
Three-day supply of K rations.
chocolate bars. powdered coffee...

603
00:56:59.800 --> 00:57:03.600
...sugar. matches. compass.
bayonet. ammunition...

604
00:57:03.900 --> 00:57:08.900
...gas mask. musette bag with ammo.
my webbing. my.45. canteen...

605
00:57:09.200 --> 00:57:13.600
...cartons of smokes. Hawkins mine.
smoke grenade. Gammon grenade...

606
00:57:14.000 --> 00:57:17.300
...TNT. this bullshit
and a pair of nasty skivvies.

607
00:57:17.600 --> 00:57:18.700
Your point?

608
00:57:18.900 --> 00:57:23.300
This weighs as much as I do. I still
got my chute. my Mae West and M-1.

609
00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:27.500
- Where are your brass knuckles?
- I could use some.

610
00:57:27.800 --> 00:57:30.300
- Sgt. Martin!
- Vest. anything for me?

611
00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:32.900
Nope. Sgt. Martin!

612
00:57:40.400 --> 00:57:41.800
Talbert!

613
00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:44.400
Floyd. Floyd M.
Here.

614
00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:48.300
- Heavy. Condoms?
- I don't know. probably.

615
00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:52.300
- What you got?
- "Dear Floyd: Give them hell."

616
00:57:52.700 --> 00:57:56.100
It's from chief of the Kokomo
Police Department.

617
00:57:59.700 --> 00:58:01.500
You gotta love cops.

618
00:58:01.700 --> 00:58:04.300
All right. listen up! Listen up!

619
00:58:04.500 --> 00:58:08.600
If you did not sign your
GI life insurance policy...

620
00:58:08.900 --> 00:58:14.100
...you go on and see Sgt. Evans
at the Headquarters Company tent.

621
00:58:14.300 --> 00:58:18.600
You boys don't let your families
miss out on $ 10.000.

622
00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:20.600
- You hear. Gerry?
- Lip!

623
00:58:20.900 --> 00:58:22.300
Yeah. boy?

624
00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:27.900
- Has Guarnere mentioned his brother?
- No.

625
00:58:28.200 --> 00:58:29.700
I got a problem.

626
00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:34.300
My wife keeps up with things at home.
Casualty lists and like that.

627
00:58:34.600 --> 00:58:37.500
- Guarnere's brother in Italy... .
- Henry?

628
00:58:37.800 --> 00:58:40.200
Killed in Monte Cassino.

629
00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:46.200
- Well. I'm sure he doesn't know.
- Damn. What do you think I should do?

630
00:58:46.700 --> 00:58:52.500
- If it were me? I'd tell him.
- A couple hours before we jump?

631
00:58:53.000 --> 00:58:54.000
I don't know.

632
00:59:03.800 --> 00:59:06.700
Why are they springing these
on us now?

633
00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:10.000
It's just 80 extra pounds
strapped to your leg.

634
00:59:10.300 --> 00:59:13.600
Does anybody have any idea
how this thing works?

635
00:59:14.600 --> 00:59:16.000
Colonel Sink.

636
00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:20.300
Colonel Sink.

637
00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:24.400
"Soldiers of the regiment:

638
00:59:24.700 --> 00:59:27.900
Tonight is the night...

639
00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:29.800
...of nights.

640
00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:34.300
As you read this. you are en route
to the adventure...

641
00:59:34.700 --> 00:59:37.900
...for which you have trained
for over two years."

642
00:59:39.100 --> 00:59:41.800
That's why they gave us ice cream.

643
00:59:44.000 --> 00:59:46.800
Easy Company! Listen up!

644
00:59:48.900 --> 00:59:51.800
Channel coast is socked in
with rain and fog.

645
00:59:52.100 --> 00:59:54.300
No jump tonight.

646
00:59:55.000 --> 01:00:00.600
The invasion has been postponed.
We're on a 24-hour stand-down.

647
01:00:32.400 --> 01:00:34.300
"Dearest Johnny"?

648
01:00:39.900 --> 01:00:42.600
I got the wrong goddamn jacket.

649
01:00:56.500 --> 01:00:58.500
"Bill Guarnere's brother... ."

650
01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:35.000
- Good luck. lads.
- See you. Tommy.

651
01:02:35.300 --> 01:02:37.100
Give Jerry one for me.

652
01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:58.500
Johnny.

653
01:03:04.700 --> 01:03:06.900
You might be looking for this.

654
01:03:07.200 --> 01:03:10.200
I took your jacket by mistake.
I'm sorry.

655
01:03:10.500 --> 01:03:12.000
You read it?

656
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:18.800
- Where the fuck is Monte Cassino?
- I don't know. Italy somewhere.

657
01:03:21.800 --> 01:03:24.300
Sorry about your brother. Bill.

658
01:03:24.600 --> 01:03:26.900
I'm sorry for my ma.

659
01:03:27.300 --> 01:03:29.000
He was... .

660
01:03:33.200 --> 01:03:34.900
Let's get this over with.

661
01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:40.000
Bill?

662
01:03:43.200 --> 01:03:45.700
I'll meet up with you over there.

663
01:03:49.400 --> 01:03:52.300
Doc Roe is giving these out
for airsickness.

664
01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:57.400
Orders are. every man takes one now.
Another. 30 minutes in the air.

665
01:03:58.100 --> 01:03:59.400
Lieutenant.

666
01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.400
2nd Platoon. listen up.

667
01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:12.700
Good luck.

668
01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:15.800
God bless you.

669
01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:20.300
I'll see you in the assembly area.

670
01:05:14.300 --> 01:05:18.900
If the wire cuts are successful.
all we should have to deal with is... .

671
01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:28.100
- Goddamn. Lieb.
- That airsick pill is making me loopy.
